{"cuisine/algerian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/algerian-cuisine","n":"Algerian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Algeria","intro":[["Algerian cuisine is influenced by Algeria's interactions and exchanges with other cultures and nations over the centuries. It is based on both land and sea products. Conquests or demographic movement towards the Algerian territory were two of the main factors of exchanges between the different peoples and cultures, introducing multiple flavors and influences, reflecting the country's history and position at the crossroads of the Mediterranean. Algerian cuisine arises from a mix of Arab, Berber, Turkish and European roots."],["Algerian cuisine offers a variety of dishes depending on the region and the season, but vegetables and cereals remain at its core. Most Algerian dishes are centered around breads, meats (lamb, beef or poultry), olive oil, vegetables, and fresh herbs. Vegetables are often used for salads, soups, tajines, couscous, and sauce-based dishes. Of all the Algerian traditional dishes available, the most famous one is Algerian couscous, recognized as a national dish."],["Pork consumption is forbidden to Muslims in Algeria, in accordance with Islamic dietary laws."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"algerian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algerian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Amour de Cuisine","ref":"site/amourdecuisine-fr","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"amourdecuisine.fr","note":"Algerian home cooking and pastries from Algerian born cook Soulef Zaoui, in English and French.","url":"https://www.amourdecuisine.fr/en","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":7,"links":[["Couscous","dish/couscous"],["Karantika","dish/karantika"],["Kourdass","dish/kourdass"],["Mbesses","dish/mbesses"],["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"],["Tajine lham-lahlou","dish/tajine-lham-lahlou"],["Zviti","dish/zviti"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"],["Besan","ingredient/besan"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Smen","ingredient/smen"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Apricots","ingredient/apricots"],["Prunes","ingredient/prunes"],["Rakhsas","ingredient/rakhsas"],["Waffle","ingredient/waffle"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/indonesian-arab-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/indonesian-arab-cuisine","n":"Indonesian Arab cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Arab Indonesian cuisine is characterized by the mixture of Middle Eastern cuisine with local Indonesian-style cuisine. Arab Indonesians brought their legacy of Arab cuisine—originally from Hadhramaut, Hejaz, Sudan and Egypt—and modified some of the dishes with the addition of Indonesian ingredients. The Arabs arrived in the Nusantara archipelago to trade and spread Islam. In Java, since the 18th century AD, most Arab traders settled on the north coast and diffused with indigenous cultures, thus affecting the local cuisine culture, especially in the use of goat and mutton meat as well as ghee in cooking."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"indonesian-arab-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesian_Arab_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/lithuanian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/lithuanian-cuisine","n":"Lithuanian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Lithuania","intro":[["Lithuanian cuisine features products suited to the cool and moist northern climate of Lithuania: barley, potatoes, rye, beets, greens, berries, and mushrooms are locally grown, and dairy products are one of its specialties. Various ways of pickling were used to preserve food for winter. Soups are widely regarded as the key to good health and are served extremely frequently. Since it shares its climate and agricultural practices with Eastern Europe, Lithuanian cuisine has much in common with its Baltic neighbors and, in general, northeastern European countries."],["Longlasting agricultural and foraging traditions along with a variety of influences during the country's history formed Lithuanian cuisine."],["German traditions have had an influence on Lithuanian cuisine, introducing pork and potato dishes, such as potato pudding (kugelis or kugel) and intestines stuffed with mashed potato (vėdarai), as well as the baroque tree cake known as šakotis. Lithuanian noblemen usually hired French chefs; French cuisine influence came to Lithuania in this way. The most exotic influence is Eastern (Karaite) cuisine, and the dish kibinai which became widely known in Lithuania. Lithuanians and other nations that were in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania also share some dishes and beverages. Lithuanian cuisine also influenced Russian and Polish cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"lithuanian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithuanian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Art of Lithuanian Cooking","ref":"cookbook/art-of-lithuanian-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Maria Gieysztor de Gorgey","note":"Hippocrene collection of traditional Lithuanian recipes.","url":"https://www.hippocrenebooks.com/store/p90/Art_of_Lithuanian_Cooking.html","suggested":true},{"name":"Baltic: New and Old Recipes from Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania","ref":"cookbook/baltic-new-and-old-recipes-from-estonia-latvia-and-lithuania","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Simon Bajada","note":"Survey of traditional and modern cooking across the three Baltic states.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":10,"links":[["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Borscht","dish/borscht"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Cold beet soup","dish/cold-beet-soup"],["Kasha","dish/kasha"],["Kibinai","dish/kibinai"],["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Šakotis","dish/sakotis"],["Uszka","dish/uszka"],["Zrazy","dish/zrazy"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Beetroot","ingredient/beetroot"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Beta vulgaris","ingredient/beta-vulgaris"],["Brassica oleracea","ingredient/brassica-oleracea"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Kefir","ingredient/kefir"],["Kvass","ingredient/kvass"],["Sorrel","ingredient/sorrel"],["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Buckwheat","ingredient/buckwheat"],["Millet","ingredient/millet"],["Oats","ingredient/oats"],["Rye","ingredient/rye"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"],["Malt","ingredient/malt"],["Rye bread","ingredient/rye-bread"],["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"dish/a16-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/a16-restaurant","n":"A16 (restaurant)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A16 is an Italian restaurant in California. There are two locations: the original location in San Francisco and a second location in Oakland. The restaurant's cuisine focuses on the Italian region of Campania. In 2014, the restaurant was named to the Top 100 Restaurants in San Francisco list by Michael Bauer of the San Francisco Chronicle."]],"src":"wikipedia","acti